Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||12-09-1999||07:55 AM||||OK, I am thinking ahead right now.  I just put in my oil pump with the shaft last night and am looking at the whole distributer connection to that oil pump shaft.<P>My old distributer ass'y (the shaft portion up to and not including the rotor and distributer cap) is looking pretty weak.  Is there a MSD setup that I should look at using?  I am definitely going to replace mine and was wondering if I should go back with original equipment or should I go with aftermarket?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Dan||12-09-1999||10:02 AM|||| Hi Boilermaker.Mske sense on what you intend to do with your car.Are you going to race it,show it,radically modify it,restore it,or what? I like your idea of inspecting associated parts during related repairs.In the long run you experience many less surprises from your baby.In all reality,the stock unit is a good unit unless you have other intentions.A high performance distributor gives you very little gain and most of that would top end.In the old days shops had distributor machines with many different springs, weights,points configurations,and fianally electronics.The mechanic also had a scope to chart out the distributor curve to recurve the distributor.In those days the stock distributor was junk,but unless you want turn 10,000 RPM or do some serious racing, I'd stay with the stock distributor.I know yor are here in town and you can try Speedway or Autozone.Best prices for parts but keep em' honest,call for prices.When we doing lunch?Anyone else wanna join us?||||reg||1
